WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.609
Let's talk a little bit about fundraising
in the 2020 campaign.

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:10.620
Incredible numbers from Bernie Sanders, the
independent Vermont Senator who is doing increasingly

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.410
well in recent polls.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:21.230
Bernie Sanders raised $25 million in January
from 648,000 different people.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:26.859
This is the most any candidate has raised
in any corridor in the election cycle period

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:31.929
and it may even be I was not able to confirm
this 100% 25 million in a month, maybe the

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:37.200
most raised by any candidate in any election
cycle ever in a single month, but fact check

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.200
me on that.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.460
I'm not totally sure about that.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.280
A teacher was the most common occupation of
people donating to Bernie Sanders.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.870
The most common employers of the donors are
Amazon, Starbucks, Walmart, and the us postal

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.290
service as well as target.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:56.830
Now, I don't know how much that really tells
us in the sense that these are just really

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:01.440
large employers and if you have a ton of employees
and there's a candidate that is resonating

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.140
with people, it sort of makes sense that there
would be a high number of individual donors

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.740
from that company.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.420
Of the 648,000 donors, 219,000 were new donors.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.200
These are people that had not previously donated
to Bernie Sanders.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:24.530
Average donation is $18 and 72 cents almost
all of Bernie's donors reportedly just a hair

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:32.270
under 100% have not yet donated the maximum
meaning that depending on how the primary

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.159
process continues to play out, there could
be significantly more money coming from these

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:38.159
donors.

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:40.890
So a few things that I think are important
to talk about.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.579
I find that the, the, the system we have is
disgusting.

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:44.579
Right?

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:47.670
You might be saying, David, how, why are you
talking about raising money?

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.729
Like it's a good, if you talk about the system
we have is terrible and we need money out

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:52.729
of politics.

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:55.490
Well until we change the game, you've got
to play the game.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.539
That's very simple and I think like 98% of
my audience understands that it would be far

00:02:00.539 --> 00:02:06.250
better that elections did not depend on fundraising
in this way or being able to, as Michael Bloomberg

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.250
is doing, pouring ungodly amounts of his own
money into this thing.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.320
It would be great if we had public financing
of election.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.500
It would be great if we made all of the reforms
to money in politics that we've been talking

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.690
about for a decade on the program.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:22.700
But right now the candidate who raises more
money tends to win and it is a good sign for

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.950
the eventual viability of a Bernie nomination
if he gets it, that he is able to raise these

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:28.950
amounts of money.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.530
So that's number one.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.620
Number two, these amounts, uh, yes, these
amounts of money.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:40.080
Number two, what is going to be Bernie strategy
as we get closer and into the super Tuesday

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.080
contention?

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.680
I've talked about how Michael Bloomberg has
made his strategy very clear.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.370
He's pouring money into super Tuesday States.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:53.810
Last week I told you I was visited by a Bloomberg
campaign staffer who came to my house and

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:59.830
uh, made it very clear that they are going
very, very hard in the super Tuesday States.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.620
When we look at that strategy, it has been
working well for Michael Bloomberg.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.330
He actually has hit 13 and 14% in some national
polls and is starting to climb in some of

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.810
those super Tuesday States.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:16.030
So given the small number of delegates in
most of these first four primaries, when you

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.600
look at South Carolina being the one with
the most delegates, but that's the one where

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.790
Bernie is actually doing the worst in.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:26.360
I would not be shocked if Bernie Sanders very,
very quickly transitions, particularly after

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.630
the New Hampshire primary on Tuesday where
he's doing really well.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:34.390
I wouldn't be shocked if Bernie transitions
into a similar strategy to Bloomberg, which

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.690
is pour the money into super Tuesday States,
both in terms of advertising on television,

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:43.400
radio, online, YouTube, et cetera, as well
as campaigning in those States.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.280
In a recall that if we look back at 2016 I
want to talk about the numbers a little bit

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.280
more.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.560
In 2016 when it was essentially a two candidate
race, Bernie versus Hillary, Bernie raised

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:03.100
21 million in January this time with multiple
candidates that are at least potentially viable.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.840
Bernie raised more $25 million.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.820
Now you have to account for, there's a larger
population now and inflation and so on, but

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.930
that is an incredibly good sign for Bernie
Sanders.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:15.480
Last thing.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.729
One of the elements that is often glossed
over and it doesn't get as much attention

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:25.030
even though it is very interesting, is the
number of people that are making multiple

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.210
donations to Bernie Sanders.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.930
He has a combination of new donors.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.490
About a third of his January donors were new
donors who hadn't donated before, but 400,000

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:41.910
people that donated to Bernie in January had
already donated to Bernie before.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:46.039
That is very important, particularly when
you look at Michael Bloomberg who has this

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:48.249
effectively bottomless pit of money.

00:04:48.249 --> 00:04:52.380
Uh, but not nearly the support that Bernie
Sanders has.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.360
It is really important that there are people
engaged enough that they are donating multiple

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.360
times.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.560
It's terrible that this is the system.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.479
It's a good thing that Bernie Sanders seems
to be doing so well.

00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:03.960
Let's take a quick break, much more to discuss
afterwards

